C(t) < 0 for gamma t/sqrt2 in (pi/4, 5pi/4): no positive Kallen-Lehmann measure, so gamma is not
an energy gap of any positive-metric Hamiltonian.

== G1. Peierls: dimerized ring, hoppings t(1 +- delta), per-site band energy, t = 1 ==
  delta  fermion gain/site  delta^2 ln(1/delta)  boson gain/particle
  0.200       3.215072e-02         6.437752e-02         0.000000e+00
  0.100       1.018181e-02         2.302585e-02         0.000000e+00
  0.050       3.091683e-03         7.489331e-03         0.000000e+00
  0.020       6.110213e-04         1.564809e-03         0.000000e+00
  0.010       1.748046e-04         4.605170e-04         0.000000e+00
  0.005       4.921599e-05         1.324579e-04         0.000000e+00
fit gain = a delta^2 ln(1/delta) + b delta^2, delta in [0.00125, 0.02]: a = 0.31812, b = 0.28305  (1/pi = 0.31831)
Fermions: gain ~ c delta^2 ln(1/delta) beats elastic cost K delta^2/2 for every K: dimerization at
any coupling (Kennedy-Lieb). Free bosons: gain exactly 0, so no instability at any K.

== G2. Landau levels in field B (charge 1), lowest E^2 at k_z = 0 ==
spin-1 charged vector (W boson / off-diagonal gluon, g-factor 2): E^2 = (2n+1)B - 2 B s_z, s_z = -1, 0(unphys.), +1
Dirac electron (g = 2):                                         E^2 = (2n+1)B - 2 B s_z, s_z = +-1/2
n=0: vector E^2/B = [3, -1]   Dirac E^2/B = [2.0, 0.0]
n=1: vector E^2/B = [5, 1]   Dirac E^2/B = [4.0, 2.0]
n=2: vector E^2/B = [7, 3]   Dirac E^2/B = [6.0, 4.0]
Vector n=0, s_z=+1: E^2 = -B < 0 (Nielsen-Olesen tachyon). Dirac minimum is 0, never negative.

